   {
    "text": "Okay, let's see. You want to know how to ride a bike, but you're not a kid. So I need to explain it in a way that's easy for someone who's just starting out, without using the words \"slow,\" \"like,\" or \"kid.\" Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, you need to get on the bike. Make sure the bike is stable and the wheels are properly locked. Then, stand up straight and push the pedals. That's the main part. You push the pedals forward and backward to make the bike move. But you have to keep your hands on the handlebars and your feet on the pedals. \n\nNext, you need to balance. When you start moving, your body will tilt a bit. Keep your eyes on the road and your feet steady. If you feel wobbly, just steady your body and keep going. You might feel a bit wobbly at first, but with practice, it'll get easier.\n\nThen, you need to steer. Use your hands to turn the handlebars. If you want to go straight, keep your hands steady. If you want to turn, gently move your hands left or right. You can also use your feet to push the pedals in different directions, but that's more for going forward. \n\nYou also need to watch where you're going. Look ahead and see where the road is. If you see a stop sign or a red light, you have to stop. But if it's a green light, you can go. Be careful of other people on the road and stay safe.\n\nNow, when you're riding, you might feel a bit dizzy or a bit wobbly, but that's normal. Just keep going and keep your head up. You can ride for a while, and you'll get better at it. Don't worry if you fall—just get back up and keep going.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the steps are: get on the bike, push the pedals, balance, steer, watch the road, and keep going. That's how you ride a bike. It's not hard, just practice and stay safe.",
